First a little history.  I've been playing Nethack for about 11-12
years now.  I started in my senior year of college which is why I had
serous concerns about graduating.  I've ascended around 50 times.  In
the first version I played there was no Moloch or Planes.  You had one
final level with a sequence of square rooms.  Each one was filled with
a particular kind of monster that you had to kill before you reached
the three rooms with the altars.  Do you know how much it sucks to
drown in the moat three feet from the altar you are trying to get to?

I say all that to say this.  I can't remember a game full of such
excitement, despair and absolute terror since that first Ascension.

For those that have gone before me I have one particular question. How
did you handle Rodney?  My Archon didn't want to touch him even under
conflict and while my method eventually worked it was very
nerve-wracking.

I had quite a bit of luck.  The best of which was finding the bones
level of one of my better previous attempts.  In that game I found a
wand of wishing in the very first room of the very first level of the
dungeon and then died eating a cockatrice egg shortly thereafter when
Anhur wouldn't help me.  Never eat unidentified eggs!  I ran into some
changes I didn't expect in 3.4.2, like not being able to cast or use a
bag while wielding a cursed unihorn but all's well that ends well I
guess.

This is the story of Peaceful the pacifist chaotic human wizardess. 
(The default on my system is female for egg laying purposes and I
forgot to change it back.)  I know I should have gone gnomish but who
wants to spend eternity two and a half feet tall?  Actually it's the
story of Peaceful version 61.  The previous sixty incarnations having
met various untimely demises or loosing pacifist conduct.  The most
distressing being Peaceful v60 who before meeting Medusa remembered to
put on her blindfold on but didn't think to take off her shield of
reflection…  Who knew?

Good Hacking!
Todd

My name is Peaceful.  I am a member of the Seekers of Peace.  I am the
61st seeker to bear this name.  The goal of our order is to recover
the Amulet of Yendor without shedding the blood (or ichor or pollen)
of a single creature.  While many mock us for attempting this
foolhardy task we are determined and will not fail though a thousand
may fall.

I didn't start out particularly well equipped for my journey.  I
entered the Dungeon of doom with a wand of digging for a few critical
escapes but no useful rings or healing potions of any kind.  I was
very tempted to leave the dungeon (alt-Q) and return when better
provisioned but decided today was as good a day as any.

I planned to descend as quickly as possible and look for shops with
useful items.  I headed for the stairs, my faithful cat Spot close
behind.  I didn't have much luck with the shops but I did scrounge up
enough gold to pay for a couple of consultations with the Oracle
gaining two levels and more than doubling my hit points.

I then made a life-changing discovery.   A statue, but it wasn't just
any statue.  This statue wore the robes of a fellow Seeker of Peace! 
On closer inspection I discovered shell fragments of an egg unlike any
I had seen before at the base of the statue.  I had a hunch that my
predecessor had eaten something very bad and, feeling the lack piety
in myself, knew the gods are often not inclined to help those that are
only nominally aligned.

Still, She had managed to stagger down the stairs and drop her
pick-axe before stonyness set in.  I was very grateful for on the
statue was a wealth of treasure. There was a magic whistle, a ring of
slow digestion, a BoH, 16k in gold and very nice armor of dwarven
manufacture.  Most importantly there was a wand of wishing.  Realizing
there was likely a scroll of charging in the bag I went ahead and used
a wish for a figurine of an  Archon.

Things went great for quite a while after that.  After a trip to mine
town and many many donations my AC was down to –19, 0 without any
armor at all.  The temple was chaotic so I worked on my luck for a
while.  This turned out to be a good idea.  I was wielding mirrors to
prevent the accidental murder of a newt or some such, and broke
several when I wasn't paying attention and ran into creatures.  I
shudder to think what would have happened on the occasions I needed
help from Anhur with low alignment and negative luck.  I also received
Magicbane for my efforts as well.

The sacrifice of a couple of human zombies led to the appearance of
Yeenoghu and Juiblex who were quickly killed by the Archon.

Deeper into the dungeon I went.  My Archon was very helpful in
liberating two potions of gain level and several extra/full healing
potions from creatures that really didn't have time to use them. 
There were also two incubi that showed more interest that was strictly
appropriate but they did teach me the fine points of the chaotic
alignment and help me reach level eleven with about 90 hit points.

Obviously, Neferet the Green was not to be convinced that I was ready
for the quest at this point.  I protested I was only going to talk to
the Dark One anyway but to no avail.  I decided to go looking for the
great castle that was rumored to be deep in the dungeon to pass the
time.  A chance-found ring of levitation made the task a little
easier.

Since the Archon had joined Spot as my protector not a single creature
had dared threaten me, or maybe they just hadn't lived long enough too
try.  This proved to be true all the way to the castle as well.  Even
the mighty Medusa perished before I could get close enough to figure
out what had happened.  I was again grateful for the contents of a
statue when I discovered a shield of reflection in the rubble of the
statue Perseus.

When I found the castle I snuck around back, crept up to the door to
the throne room and blew the magic whistle until the Archon appeared
on the other side.  For long minutes there was the sound of battle and
bloodshed from beyond the door but everything eventually fell quiet.

I quickly retrieved my second wand of wishing from the tower and
retreated back to mine town.  Once there I wished for a wand of
polymorph and went to work on all the stuff I had collected thus far.

For my effort I raised all my stats to 18, advanced to level 15, had
close to 200 hit points and got boots of speed.  On the down side when
I was sorting through all the stuff I accidentally dropped my own
shield and Magicbane into the piles and destroyed them.  Argggg.

Since I was tired of searching for magic armor I used more wishes to
replace the shield and added a helm of telepathy and GDSM.  I also
wished for a replacement athame.  In retrospect this was probably a
squandering of my resources but I was in a hurry to test my skills
against the creatures of Gehennom

On the way there I stopped off at the lonely tower and told Neferet I
was now more than ready to go after Eye of the Aethiopica.  He agreed
and I headed into the Dark Tower to confront the Dark one.  I tried to
reason with him but he refused to listen.  Alas, sometimes when words
fail swords must serve.  More specifically, Sunsword.  The Archon
angered by the Dark Ones disregard struck again and again until there
was nothing left but a cloud of dust.

Once my obligation to my old mentor was satisfied I resumed my trek
into the depths of Gehennom.  Not wishing to fight I gave Asmodeus and
Baalzebub the gold they demanded though it seemed strange that the
demon lords would abandon their lairs for a paltry 13 and 17 coins.

I found the stair to Vlad's tower shortly after that.  I never got a
chance to meet Vlad who must have been out visiting relatives.  My
Archon had been searching ahead of me when he suddenly came around the
corner and handed me the Candelabrum of Invocation.  When I asked
where he got it the Archon said he had found it just lying around and
that we should leave quickly.

It wasn't until I reached Orcus' town that I ran into trouble.  My
Archon, who had up until that point defended me tirelessly, absolutely
refused to touch him!  He would approach and then cower away in fear. 
Even wearing my ring of conflict only caused the Archon to take a
listless strike every once in a while doing little damage.  I pointed
out that as an Angelic being he had the force of right on his side and
should have no fear of death.  He pointed out that this was the most
powerful being he had ever seen and it wasn't me who was doing all the
fighting.

I decided not to press the matter and made a dash for the stairs. 
Orcus dogged my steps appearing ahead of me every time I thought I'd
lost him, summoning liches, dragons and mind flayers.  I wasn't
terribly worried.  Anything I couldn't charm had a tendency to
attacked by its former friends who were now determined to clear my
path.

Then tragedy struck.  With a loud Burrrrp!  My Archon was gone.  A
trapper had consumed him in one bite.  Overcome with sorrow I finally
made it to the stairs ahead of Orcus and escaped down to the next
level.  I had gained the allegiance of several dragons but they could
not replace my missing friend.

I finally reached to bottom of the dungeon where to my surprise I
immediately found the vibrating square.  After marking the spot I
returned to the strange portal I had found several levels above and
entered the wizards tower.  I realized great danger was ahead so I
used one of my remaining 5 wishes to summon another Archon.  As if
enraged by the fate of his previous brother the Archon cut a swath of
destruction through the residents of the tower and I was soon standing
outside the inner sanctum of the Wizard of Yendor.

Realizing my current pet would probably have the same problem with the
Wizard as my previous one had with Orcus I formulated another plan.  I
slipped on my rings of polymorph and polymorph control and assumed the
form of a water nymph.  Perhaps I would be able to steal what I could
not take by force.  I broke through the wall and the wizard teleported
to me immediately.  I tried to steal the book four, five, six times
but each time was denied.  Finally he landed a headache spell that
caused me to revert to my true form and I was forced to flee.

I am still unsure what caused my plan to fail but given that it had I
finally managed to come up with another.  If my Archon would not
attack because the wizard was too powerful perhaps I could weaken him.
 I could not bring myself to kill the wizard but using the polymorph
trick I could become a vampire lord and drain him to the point where
my pet no longer feared him.

It worked like a charm.  Drained of three levels the wizard looked
like a more suitable target and the Archon soon presented me with the
book of the dead.  I used a cursed scroll to teleport back to the
bottom of the dungeon and headed for the vibrating square.

It occurs to me that it is sometimes the smallest things that can kick
off a near fatal chain of events.  For me I was a simple magic trap. 
I had almost reached the square when a flash of light blinded me.  I
reached for my unicorn horn to cure the blindness and received a nasty
surprise, instead of healing me the horn caused me to start
hallucinating! Apparently the wizard had managed to curse it before he
had fallen.

Grumbling, I wielded the horn and prepared to cast remove curse.  What
I didn't realize is that I would be unable to cast effectively with my
hand welded to the horn!  Worse, when I reached for my bag to retrieve
some holy water I couldn't even maneuver well enough to do that.

I began to panic a little.  I couldn't cast spells and everything I
could think of to help me with the problem was inaccessibly tucked
away in my bag.  Being in Gehennom I couldn't pray.  I couldn't take
off my GDSM to use a polymorph or level teleport trap even if I could
find one.  Juiblex's level was between me and anyplace I could pray
and I had no means of levitating or freezing the water.  To top it all
off I knew the wizard would soon be on my trail again and without
being able to change my rings I had no way to handle him.  Just when I
was about to despair I remembered a little used ability of the Eye of
the Aethiopica.  I breathed a sigh of relief as I activated it and was
transported to the gnomish mines.

Looking back I could probably have made my way back to Vlad's tower
but I had forgotten that, while in Gehennom, his tower is not actually
part of it.  Anhur looked favorably on my request for help and I was
back in business.  As it turned out this little detour might have
saved me some trouble.  No sooner had I finished my prayer than the
wizard appeared again and this time he had brought his evil twin. 
Chuckling, I teleported to the stairwell, whistled up my Archon and
escaped before he could reach me.

I teleported back to the first level of Gehennom and decided to walk
back to the bottom just to make sure my path was clear.  I had lost
memories of several levels to mind flayers when I released the wizard
and wanted a chance to remap them before I had the amulet.

This was a mistake.  When I returned to Orcus Town he was all over me
and this time had summoned at least three arch-liches to help.  I had
just about reached the stairs when I heard that dreaded sound again. 
Burrrrp!  My second Archon just joined my first one in the exact same
trapper.  I had planned to go genoless but his was just too much!  In
a fit of genocidal rage I committed, well, genocide.  Never again will
trappers or lurkers trouble the world.  And since an evil act one
committed seems easier to repeat I wiped out all Ls as well.

I jumped down the stairs only to realize Orcus had managed to follow
me.  He would summon more creatures and I would charm them.  Then a
strange thing happened.  He just stopped.  Stepping closer I
determined that one of my attempts at charming his pets had actually
worked somewhat on him.  While not an ally he was at least peaceful. 
I used one of my dwindling supply of wishes to summon yet another
Archon and continued on.

I made it to the vibrating square for the last time.  I performed the
ceremony of invocation and shaking with anticipation headed down take
the Amulet of Yendor from the High Priest of Moloch.  The dragons and
the Archon made short work of the undead between me and the inner
sanctum.  I used the whistle trick to get the Archon on the other side
of the wall and let him deal with the lesser priests.

I had thought to use vampiric abilities and the Archon to handle the
high priest but that proved to be unnecessary.  Just as I was about to
enter the sanctum a group of monsters appeared around me including a
purple worm.  I quickly charmed them and realized that the worm was
well suited to the task of recovering the amulet.  I displaced him
into the sanctum put on my ring of conflict and watched as a Burrrrp!
went in  my favor for once.  I snatched the Amulet of Yendor from the
ground and headed for the stairs.

I was hit with a curse as I exited the sanctum.  I nearly fell under
the crushing weight of my now cursed bag of holding and could barely
move.  It was so heavy I couldn't cast or read remove curse or even
dip it into the holy water I had begun carrying after my encounter
with the cursed unicorn horn.  I dropped the bag to the floor. 
Knowing that cancellation was my only option I reached into the bag
with great trepidation.

My fears were realized.  Several of the items I had placed in the bag
were gone.  The worst were my blessed scrolls of charging and the wand
of wishing (1:1).  Making the best of it I took the discharged wand
and obtained a wand of cancellation with my last wish.  I really
shouldn't complain with all the wishes I had already received but it
hurts to loose the safety net they provide.

I met the wizard twice again on the way up.  Each encounter was more
difficult as he became more powerful every time he was killed.  The
last time he appeared before I escaped Gehennom he managed to steal
every artifact I had.  I was forced to retreat and renew my vampire
form at least three times before he was reduced to the point that my
Archon would take up the fight.  Those headache spells can force you
back into human form quickly!

I finally reached the stairs and exited the Dungeons of Doom onto the
Plane of Earth.  As expected the wizard was there waiting for me.  Not
so expected, he double troubled the very first turn of combat. 
Applying my stethoscope I discovered he had grown powerful since our
battles had begun.  In his current incarnations he was level 37 and
level 35.  Since I had to get him down to 28 before I could hope for
help from my pet I had a hard fight ahead.

Things did not go well.  I carved a narrow tunnel out of the rock so
that there was only one square I could be attacked from.  The problem
was that his headache spell could force me out of vampire form in
about two hits.  Since there was no way to retreat I couldn't hide to
wait out another polymorph from my ring.  I had already charged my
wand of polymorph three times and had two charges left.  After using
these I still needed to drain about 10 levels from the wizards.

With some trepidation I used an uncursed scroll of charging and some
holy water to recharge the wand for the fourth time.  Luck was with me
and it didn't explode.  Looking at the wand I realized that the 4
charges I had gained were unlikely to be enough and I was probably
going to die here.

As it turned out I didn't need the wand after all as the wizard
provided the means of his own destruction.   I was standing at the end
of the hall with both wizards lined up next to me followed by my
Archon when suddenly a black dragon appeared from nowhere.  (DA@@V) He
breathed disintegration.  The bolt missed my Archon struck one of the
wizards bounced off my shield and killed the second!  I used my
crystal ball to find the portal and quickly made for the exit before
any further catastrophes could occur.

The other elemental planes were much easier.  I had saved up several
scrolls of scare monster for the plane of air and any elementals that
didn't run away fast enough were killed by the Archon.  Earth was just
a straight shot to the portal levitating over the lava.

The first thing I did when I entered the plane of water was genocide ;
 Not so much because they were a threat but just because I didn't want
to take the time to deal with them.  One last use of the crystal ball
found me the portal and I had at last reached the astral plane.

At this point I had only two pets, the Archon and a guardian angel. 
With them in tow I headed for the temple guarded by Death.  Rather
than fight or even wait for my friends to clear my path I drained
three wands of teleportation to reach the altar quickly sending its
defenders to other temples.  It was, of course the wrong altar.  I got
tagged a couple time by Death and lost 20+ hit points on the way back
out but picked up lots of pets when monsters appeared from nowhere and
priests added bugs to the mix.

Next I tried Pestilence.  The Archon, angel and dragons fought my
enemies while the insects provided a barrier to the rider.  I managed
to slip by only to discover that I had arrived yet again at the wrong
temple.  The only thing I had to show for my trouble was more pets.  A
group of storm giants appeared just as I was heading for Famine
allowing me to trap the other two riders in their areas.

The trip to the last alter was a combination of the previous two
attempts.  Anything that got too close I would teleport away and my
pets killed everything else.  I reached the altar of Anhur without
being touched.  I had really done it!  I was standing on the altar
holding the Amulet of Yendor!  Thankfully, I offered the amulet to
Anhur and....

  

